From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 02:30:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: dispatch two dependent categorical variables to a real
 2D plot

X, Y = RV(ProbabilitySpace(event_sim)) (each draw returning a pair of
strings, e.g. one categorical variable's outcome depending on another's)
produced a nonsensical plot: (X & Y).sim(n).plot() drew each pair as a
meaningless two-point line against index (0, 1), titled "Sample Path".

The cause: RVResults.dim is only computed for numeric results (see
RVResults.__init__), so a Tuple of two strings gets dim=None. plot()
already has a dedicated branch for 1D categorical data for exactly this
reason (_is_categorical_1d), but had no 2D counterpart, so a categorical
pair fell through every dim==1/dim==2/1D-categorical check straight to
the generic ensemble path-plot branch meant for TimeFunction results.

Added _is_categorical_2d() (mirroring _is_categorical_1d) and a new
dispatch branch that routes such a pair to type="tile" (default),
"mosaic", or "stackedbar" -- the three renderers that already accept
raw categorical arrays directly. A categorical scatter is deliberately
not offered: make_scatter's jitter modes ("spiral"/"bins") assume
integer-coded positions, and extending them to categorical data is
future work rather than something to work around here; requesting
type="scatter" (or marginal=True, whose layout code is similarly
untested against string axes) now raises a clear, actionable error
instead of an obscure crash further down.

Note: "tile" is the default for both small and large samples (unlike
plain 2D_dd's small-n default of "scatter", not offered here). Whether
"mosaic" should instead be the default for this case -- the original
suggestion -- is intentionally left as an open question; see the
accompanying summary document.

Regression tests added in test_plot.py, including the exact
event_sim example this was reported against.
